Notebook[{Cell["TMD beam and soft functions, step 2 (FeynCalc route): squared \
matrix elements", "Title"], Cell["Input: the amplitudes of step 1 \
(Results/Amplitudes.wl).  Output: Results/FeynCalc/Algebra.wl with the \
squared operator matrix elements, summed over spins and colours, as functions \
of the momentum fraction x and the transverse momentum kT2 of the emitted \
parton.  The FORM and Tracer routes (2-Algebra-FORM.wl, 2-Algebra-Tracer.wl) \
produce the same file from the same amplitudes.\n\nWhat is squared.  An \
operator matrix element <X| O |p> is the open amplitude of step 1 with the \
operator leg \"O\" carrying the operator's open index.  Its square, summed \
over the spins and colours of X and averaged over those of p, is closed with \
the operator's own structure:\n\n   quark operator:  M2_q = 1/(2 n.p) * \
(1/2)(1/Nc) Sum Tr[ n-slash . Gamma . rho(p) . Gammabar ]       rho(p) = \
p-slash (incoming quark) or the gluon polarization sum,\n   gluon operator:  \
M2_g = 1/(x (n.p)^2) * (1/(D-2))(1/(Nc^2-1)) Sum (-g_{mu nu}) M^mu \
M^nu*,\n\nwhere n-slash is the gamma^+ of the operator qbar gamma^+ q, -g_{mu \
nu} contracts the two F^{+ mu} of the gluon operator, and the prefactors 1/(2 \
n.p) and 1/(x (n.p)^2) come from the Fourier transform in b^- that defines \
the beam function (docs/CONVENTIONS.md).  With these normalizations the Born \
matrix elements are exactly 1, which is the statement B = delta(1-x) at \
lowest order; the real-emission matrix elements, integrated over the \
one-parton phase space with the longitudinal constraint n.k = (1-x) n.p (step \
5), give the NLO beam functions.\n\nKinematics.  n^2 = nb^2 = 0, n.nb = 2; \
p^2 = k^2 = 0; n.p = pn; n.k = (1-x) pn; and k_T, the transverse momentum of \
k with respect to p and n, satisfies kT2 = (n.k)(nb.k) and p.k = \
kT2/(2(1-x)).  External gluons are summed with the physical polarization sum \
d^{mu nu}(k) = -g^{mu nu} + (k^mu n^nu + n^mu k^nu)/(n.k) (light-cone gauge, \
reference vector n); the Ward identities of step 1 guarantee that the result \
does not depend on this choice, which is checked below for one channel.  The \
soft matrix element is a function of n.k and nb.k only.", "Text"], 
  Cell["Load", "Section"], Cell["Get[FileNameJoin[{NotebookDirectory[], \
\"..\", \"..\", \"FeynFacet\", \"FeynFacet.wl\"}]]", "Input"], 
  Cell["LoadFeynCalc[];\namplitudes = \
LoadAmplitudes[][\"FeynCalc\"];\nKeys[amplitudes]", "Input"], 
  Cell["Kinematics and polarization sums", "Section"], 
  Cell["FCClearScalarProducts[];\nSPD[p] = 0; SPD[k] = 0; SPD[n] = 0; SPD[nb] \
= 0; SPD[n, nb] = 2;\nSPD[p, n] = pn; SPD[k, n] = (1 - x) pn; SPD[p, k] = \
kT2/(2 (1 - x)); SPD[k, nb] = kT2/((1 - x) pn); SPD[p, nb] = 0;\neikonal = \
FAEikonal[mom_, v_] :> 1/ExpandScalarProduct[SPD[v, mom]];\n(* physical \
polarization sum of a gluon with momentum l, reference vector n \
*)\npolarizationSum[l_, mu_, nu_] := -MTD[mu, nu] + (FVD[l, mu] FVD[n, nu] + \
FVD[n, mu] FVD[l, nu])/SPD[l, n];\npolarizationSum[k, mu, nu]", "Input"], 
  Cell["square[amplitude, conjugate, ...] multiplies an amplitude by the \
conjugate of another, replaces every polarization vector by an index and \
inserts the polarization sums, closes the Dirac chains with the spin sums, \
contracts the specified polarization tensors before expanding the traces, and \
performs the colour sum. The polarization tensors and their dimensional \
prescription are unchanged; only the order of the contractions is different.  \
The operator leg is treated as follows: for the quark operator the \
placeholder spinor ubar(q) ... u(q) of the two factors is replaced by n-slash \
(operatorSpinSum); for the gluon operator the placeholder polarization \
vectors epsilon(q) epsilon*(q) are replaced by -g_{mu nu}.", "Text"], 
  Cell["(* the operator placeholder spinor ubar(q) is renamed, so that the \
slash that\n   FermionSpinSum produces for it can be recognized and replaced \
by n-slash;\n   the external massless fermions get their momentum-slash as \
usual *)\noperatorQuark = Spinor[Momentum[q, D], 0, 1] -> \
Spinor[Momentum[qOperator, D], 0, 1];\noperatorSpinSum = \
DiracGamma[Momentum[qOperator, D], D] :> GSD[n];\nsquare[amp_, conj_, \
polarizationRules_, sums_:1] := Module[{expr},\n  expr = (Total[amp] /. \
operatorQuark) ComplexConjugate[Total[conj] /. operatorQuark] /. eikonal;\n  \
expr = expr /. polarizationRules // FeynAmpDenominatorExplicit;\n  expr = \
FermionSpinSum[expr] /. operatorSpinSum;\n  expr = Contract[expr sums] // \
ExpandScalarProduct;\n  expr = DiracSimplify[expr, DiracTraceEvaluate -> \
True] // Contract // ExpandScalarProduct;\n  expr = SUNSimplify[expr, \
Explicit -> True, SUNNToCACF -> False] // Contract;\n  Factor[Together[expr \
/. SUNN -> Nc]]];\n(* the colour sums are written through CF = (Nc^2-1)/(2 \
Nc) and CA = Nc at the\n   end; the gluon normalization Tr(T^a T^b) = TF \
delta^{ab} with TF = 1/2 is FeynCalc's *)\ncolourFactors[expr_] := \
Factor[expr] /. (-1 + Nc) (1 + Nc) -> 2 Nc CF /. Nc -> CA;", "Input"], 
  Cell["Born matrix elements: normalization", "Section"], 
  Cell["Quark: Tr[n-slash p-slash]/(2 n.p) with the spin average 1/2 and the \
colour average 1/Nc of delta_ii = Nc.  Gluon: the amplitude is gOp (n.p \
eps^mu - p^mu n.eps); contracted with -g_{mu nu}, summed over the physical \
polarizations (which gives (D-2) (n.p)^2) and averaged with \
1/((D-2)(Nc^2-1)), divided by (n.p)^2 (x = 1).", "Text"], 
  Cell["bornQ = square[amplitudes[\"BornQ\"], amplitudes[\"BornQ\"], {}] /. x \
-> 1;\nbornQ = Factor[bornQ (1/2) (1/Nc)/(2 pn)/gOp^2];\nbornG = \
square[amplitudes[\"BornG\"], amplitudes[\"BornG\"],\n  \
{Momentum[Polarization[q, -I], D] -> LorentzIndex[mu, D], \
Momentum[Polarization[q, I], D] -> LorentzIndex[nu, D],\n   \
Momentum[Polarization[p, I], D] -> LorentzIndex[al, D], \
Momentum[Polarization[p, -I], D] -> LorentzIndex[be, D]}];\nbornG = \
Factor[Contract[bornG (-MTD[mu, nu]) polarizationSum[p, al, be]] /(D - \
2)/(Nc^2 - 1)/pn^2/gOp^2];\nRequire[bornQ === 1 && bornG === 1, \"The Born \
matrix elements are not normalized to 1\", {bornQ, bornG}];\n{bornQ, bornG}", 
   "Input"], Cell["Real emission: the four beam channels", "Section"], 
  Cell["q -> q (gluon emitted): incoming quark averaged with 1/2 and 1/Nc, \
the emitted gluon summed with the physical polarization sum.  The chains are \
closed with n-slash (operator) and p-slash (incoming quark).", "Text"], 
  Cell["gluonK = {Momentum[Polarization[k, -I], D] -> LorentzIndex[mu, D], \
Momentum[Polarization[k, I], D] -> LorentzIndex[nu, D]};\ngluonP = \
{Momentum[Polarization[p, I], D] -> LorentzIndex[al, D], \
Momentum[Polarization[p, -I], D] -> LorentzIndex[be, D]};\noperatorG = \
{Momentum[Polarization[q, -I], D] -> LorentzIndex[rho, D], \
Momentum[Polarization[q, I], D] -> LorentzIndex[sigma, D]};\n\nrealQQ = \
square[amplitudes[\"BeamQQ\"], amplitudes[\"BeamQQ\"], gluonK, \
polarizationSum[k, mu, nu]] (1/2) (1/Nc)/(2 pn)/gOp^2 // Factor", "Input"], 
  Cell["g -> q (the antiquark is emitted): incoming gluon averaged with \
1/(D-2) and 1/(Nc^2-1), summed with the physical polarization sum; the \
outgoing antiquark's spin sum is k-slash.", "Text"], 
  Cell["realQG = square[amplitudes[\"BeamQG\"], amplitudes[\"BeamQG\"], \
gluonP, polarizationSum[p, al, be]]/(D - 2)/(Nc^2 - 1)/(2 pn)/gOp^2 // \
Factor", "Input"], Cell["q -> g (the quark is emitted; gluon operator): \
incoming quark averaged with 1/2 and 1/Nc; the operator indices are \
contracted with -g_{rho sigma}; the prefactor is 1/(x (n.p)^2).", "Text"], 
  Cell["realGQ = square[amplitudes[\"BeamGQ\"], amplitudes[\"BeamGQ\"], \
operatorG, -MTD[rho, sigma]] (1/2) (1/Nc)/(x pn^2)/gOp^2 // Factor", 
   "Input"], Cell["g -> g: both gluons with physical polarization sums, the \
incoming one averaged with 1/((D-2)(Nc^2-1)).", "Text"], 
  Cell["realGG = square[amplitudes[\"BeamGG\"], amplitudes[\"BeamGG\"], \
Join[gluonK, gluonP, operatorG],\n  polarizationSum[k, mu, nu] \
polarizationSum[p, al, be] (-MTD[rho, sigma])]/(D - 2)/(Nc^2 - 1)/(x \
pn^2)/gOp^2 // Factor", "Input"], Cell["All four are proportional to gs^2/kT2 \
times a function of x and D only: the dependence on n.p has cancelled, as it \
must by boost invariance along the beam.  Written as gs^2 K_ij(x, D)/kT2, the \
K_ij are the D-dimensional real-emission splitting functions times (1-x):\n\n \
  K_qq/(1-x) = 2 CF [(1+x^2)/(1-x) - eps (1-x)],   K_qg/(1-x) = 2 TF [1 - \
2x(1-x)/(1-eps)],   K_gq/(1-x) = 2 CF [(1+(1-x)^2)/x - eps x],   K_gg/(1-x) = \
4 CA [x/(1-x) + (1-x)/x + x(1-x)],\n\nwith D = 4 - 2 eps and TF = 1/2.  At \
eps = 0 they are the LO splitting functions in the normalization alpha_s/(4 \
Pi) (twice the usual ones).", "Text"], Cell["real = colourFactors /@ <|\"qq\" \
-> realQQ, \"qg\" -> realQG, \"gq\" -> realGQ, \"gg\" -> \
realGG|>;\nRequire[FreeQ[real, pn], \"The real matrix elements depend on \
n.p\", real];\nkernels = Map[Factor[# kT2/gs^2] &, \
real];\nRequire[FreeQ[kernels, kT2], \"The real matrix elements are not \
proportional to 1/kT2\"];\nexpected = <|\"qq\" -> 2 CF ((1 + x^2)/(1 - x) - \
eps (1 - x)), \"qg\" -> 2 (1/2) (1 - 2 x (1 - x)/(1 - eps)),\n  \"gq\" -> 2 \
CF ((1 + (1 - x)^2)/x - eps x), \"gg\" -> 4 CA (x/(1 - x) + (1 - x)/x + x (1 \
- x))|>;\nRequire[And @@ Table[Simplify[kernels[c]/(1 - x) /. D -> 4 - 2 eps] \
=== Simplify[expected[c]], {c, Keys[kernels]}],\n  \"The real-emission \
kernels differ from the D-dimensional splitting functions\", \
kernels];\nTableForm[Normal[kernels], TableHeadings -> {None, {\"channel\", \
\"kT2 M2/gs^2\"}}]", "Input"], Cell["Check of gauge invariance: the qq matrix \
element computed with -g^{mu nu} as polarization sum of the emitted gluon \
(allowed for a single external gluon when the Ward identity holds) is the \
same.", "Text"], Cell["realQQFeynman = square[amplitudes[\"BeamQQ\"], \
amplitudes[\"BeamQQ\"], gluonK, -MTD[mu, nu]] (1/2) (1/Nc)/(2 pn)/gOp^2 // \
Factor;\nRequire[Simplify[realQQFeynman - realQQ] === 0, \"The qq matrix \
element depends on the gluon polarization sum\", {realQQ, realQQFeynman}];", 
   "Input"], Cell["The soft matrix element", "Section"], 
  Cell["The soft current J^mu = gs T^a [n^mu/(n.k) - nb^mu/(nb.k)] \
(fundamental) or the same with the adjoint generator.  Its square with -g_{mu \
nu} (one external gluon), the colour trace Tr(T^a T^a) = CF Nc divided by Nc \
for the normalization Tr(1)/Nc = 1 of the soft function, gives 4 gs^2 \
CF/((n.k)(nb.k)); the adjoint line gives CA instead of CF.  Here the \
invariants n.k = kn and nb.k = kb are kept separate (the soft function has no \
longitudinal constraint).", "Text"], Cell["FCClearScalarProducts[];\nSPD[k] = \
0; SPD[n] = 0; SPD[nb] = 0; SPD[n, nb] = 2; SPD[k, n] = kn; SPD[k, nb] = \
kb;\nsoftQ = square[amplitudes[\"SoftQ\"], amplitudes[\"SoftQ\"], gluonK, \
-MTD[mu, nu]]/Nc/gSoft^2 // Factor;\nsoftG = square[amplitudes[\"SoftG\"], \
amplitudes[\"SoftG\"], gluonK, -MTD[mu, nu]]/(Nc^2 - 1)/gSoft^2 // \
Factor;\nsoft = colourFactors /@ <|\"q\" -> softQ, \"g\" -> softG|>", 
   "Input"], Cell["Require[Simplify[soft[\"q\"] - 4 gs^2 CF/(kn kb)] === 0 && \
Simplify[soft[\"g\"] - 4 gs^2 CA/(kn kb)] === 0, \"Soft matrix elements \
differ from 4 gs^2 C_i/((n.k)(nb.k))\", soft];", "Input"], 
  Cell["Save", "Section"], Cell["algebra = <|\"Born\" -> <|\"q\" -> bornQ, \
\"g\" -> bornG|>, \"Real\" -> real, \"Soft\" -> \
soft|>;\nSaveResult[\"Algebra\", algebra, \"FeynCalc\"];\nKeys[algebra]", 
   "Input"]}, WindowSize -> {1000, 800}]
